most people in pakistan live along the  indus river it runs from the north to  the south and is an area prone to severe  monsoon flooding but this year's monsoon  rains are unprecedented affecting all  four of the country's provinces  if we look at how many homes have been  damaged this shows which districts are  the worst affected the darker areas have  the greatest damage up to  000 130.000 have been damaged in these  different districts  now satellite imagery shows the extent  of the flooding on the left here  villages along a section of the indus  river that was back in march this is the  same location now look completely  submerged and that's why the navy has  been brought in to help these remote  rural areas  global warming is being blamed let's  just consider glaciers there are over  7000 in pakistan that's more than  anywhere else outside the polar regions  but rising temperatures are causing  increasing glacial melt which in turn is  causing flooding  and that isn't the only problem climate  change is increasing the intensity the  frequency and the duration of monsoon  rainfalls this heat map shows how much  rain will fall during this year's  monsoon and it's all above normal levels  but particularly in the darker blue  areas the sindh and the punjab provinces  and this chart gives us a sense of how  vulnerable pakistan is to climate change  it shows how much hotter the country  could get the two lines show the  projected number of hot days that's days  over 35 degrees that pakistan could see  over the next 80 years the blue line is  the best case scenario that's if rapid  global action is taken to cut greenhouse  gas emissions but if little action is  taken the orange line here shows the  worst case scenario the number of hot  days leaps from just under  130 to around 180  a year and that could be catastrophic  causing drought  failing crops and significant problems  with water supplies
 

